course: everyday
lesson: 365
title: Confession, prayer and making amends at church
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: In the spring Nathan in Cork pretended to be ill to miss church, lied about his prayers, and drew on the hymn book. In the end he owned up to all of it, went to confession, said sorry to the churchwarden (a helper who looks after the church) and paid for a new hymn book from his pocket money. He walked out of church feeling as light as a balloon and was sure he would never do anything wrong again.
explain: A few weeks later, bored and lazy after school, he was egged on by older boys and threw a firecracker at old Mrs Doyle's door. Her heart raced, her dog hid under the bed for a whole day, and the porch boards were scorched. A police officer came round and gave Nathan a warning, Dad paid for the repair, and for a while the neighbours looked at him differently. The shame was worse than before.
explain: Nathan went to confession again, ashamed that he was back so soon. The priest said: «Everyone slips. Forgiveness doesn't run out. But each time, you must put it right.» Mum said the same in her own way, and added: «Habits change slowly — fewer silly friends, and more to do than to sit about idle.» Nathan said sorry to Mrs Doyle at her door, painted her porch with Dad over two Saturdays, and walked her dog after school for a month. Later he joined the football club.
explain: Other children at the same church made smaller slips. Freya said she had said her prayers when she hadn't; when Mum asked what she had prayed for, she could not answer and owned up. Max pulled faces at the baby during a christening (the service when a baby is welcomed into the church) and spoiled the photos; he said sorry to the baby's mum and drew the baby a card. Leah sneaked off to the bouncy castle in her turn to help at the church fair, so she said sorry and stayed to clear up at the end. Ruby laughed out loud when the vicar (the priest) mixed up his words; when she said sorry, he laughed and said he does it a lot.
explain: Confession brings real relief and forgiveness. It does not make a person perfect, and it never replaces saying sorry and making amends — sometimes with words, sometimes with work.
fact: Being forgiven in confession does not mean a person will never do wrong again; most people slip back sometimes.
fact: In Christian teaching, God's forgiveness does not run out for someone who is truly sorry.
fact: After a second wrong, putting it right still matters as much as the first time.
fact: Making amends can be done with work, such as painting, cleaning or helping, not only with words.
fact: Fireworks and firecrackers thrown at people or houses can cause burns, fires and great fright, especially to old people and animals.
fact: The police can give young people a warning for antisocial behaviour, and parents may have to pay for damage.
fact: Idle time with friends who egg you on makes trouble more likely; clubs and hobbies help change habits.
fact: Prayer is a quiet talk with God, not a box to tick.
fact: Mocking what someone holds dear, such as their faith, to upset them is unkind.
fact: Things borrowed from church, like prayer books or hymn books, are shared and need care.
fact: Trust lost with neighbours comes back slowly, through steady good behaviour.
exercise: After his first confession, what did Nathan wrongly believe? | That he would never do anything wrong again | That he would have to come back and confess every single day | That the hymn book was his | That the priest was cross with him
exercise: Why did Nathan throw the firecracker? | He was bored and egged on by older boys | Mrs Doyle had asked him to make some noise for the holiday | He thought it was a toy | He was celebrating a holiday
exercise: Which harm did the firecracker cause? | Mrs Doyle's fright, a scared dog and a scorched porch | No harm, as it was only a noise | Only a little puff of smoke that soon blew away down the street | Harm only to Nathan's hand
exercise: What were the consequences for Nathan and his family? | A police warning and Dad paying for the repair | A reward from the neighbours | A fine paid by Mrs Doyle for having such a noisy dog | Nothing, because nobody was hurt
exercise: What did the priest say when Nathan came back so soon? | Everyone slips; forgiveness doesn't run out; put it right | You cannot be forgiven twice for the same sort of thing, Nathan | Come back when you are perfect | Confession only works once a year
exercise: How did Nathan make amends to Mrs Doyle? | Said sorry, painted her porch and walked her dog | Sent a card and kept away from her | Asked Dad to go round and say sorry to her for him | Bought her a firework of her own
exercise: What did Mum say helps habits change? | Fewer silly friends and more things to do | Staying in his room for the whole of the summer holidays | Never going outside again | Promising harder each time
exercise: Why did Nathan join the football club? | To have more to do than sit about idle | Because the older boys from the street were there too | To get away from Mrs Doyle's barking dog | Because the priest ordered him to go there
exercise: Freya said she had said her prayers when she had not. Mum asked what she prayed for. What happened? | She could not answer and owned up | She made up a long prayer on the spot | Mum did not care either way and said it was fine | Freya said her prayers were a secret
exercise: Max pulled faces at the baby during a christening, and the photos were spoiled. What put it right? | Saying sorry to the baby's mum and drawing the baby a card | Saying the photographer was far too slow with the camera | Pulling a nicer face next time | Offering to take new photos himself
exercise: Leah sneaked off to the bouncy castle when it was her turn to help at the church fair. What made up for it? | Saying sorry and staying to clear up at the end | Bringing the others a balloon | Saying she had already helped earlier in the day | Asking for her turn to be next year
exercise: Owen told his gran he did not believe in God just to upset her. Why was that unkind? | He used what she holds dear to hurt her | Grans should not believe in God | Saying out loud what you really think is always unkind | It was only a joke so it was fine
exercise: Ava took the children's prayer book home and lost it. What made it right? | Telling the teacher and buying a new one | Saying the book was never there in the first place | Sharing someone else's book | Hiding until they forget
exercise: Ruby laughed out loud when the vicar mixed up his words. What did the vicar say when she said sorry? | He laughed and said he does it a lot | He said she could not come back to church again | He asked her to read a lesson instead | He did not answer her and walked away
exercise: Nathan's older friends say «nobody died, it was only a banger». What is true? | It caused real fright, damage and danger | It was just harmless fun, so nobody should mind | Fireworks never hurt anyone | Only the dog was bothered
exercise: Nathan felt ashamed to go back to confession so soon. Which thought is wiser? | It is better to go back honestly than to stay away | He should wait until he is perfect | He should go to a different priest so nobody knows | He should never confess again
test: older lads: «go on, chuck it at her door, it'll be a laugh». What's the right answer? | No, it could hurt her and her dog; I'm off | OK but only a small one | OK if you go first | OK, but only if she'll never know it was us
test: «I got away with a banger at the old lady's last Halloween, it worked last time, so it's fine». Is it? | No, it scared her and could have caused a fire | Yes, if no one caught you last time, it's fine | Yes, at Halloween it's allowed | Only if she has no dog
test: Priest: «Weren't you here just last month?» Nathan nods, red. What's the best thing to say? | «Yes, Father. I slipped again and I'm sorry» | «No, that was my brother» | «I'm only here for Mum» | «I don't need to be forgiven this time, Father»
test: Mum: «Being forgiven doesn't mean the porch paints itself». What does she mean? | He still has to make amends with work | The porch will fix itself | He's not really forgiven until the porch is painted | Painting is a punishment from God
test: «Mrs Doyle won't even look at me any more». What will help most? | Steady good behaviour over time, and the work he promised | Avoiding her street for good | Telling her it wasn't really that bad at all, honestly | Sending her a firework as a gift
test: Gran, very hurt: «You don't mean that, do you?» after Owen said he doesn't believe. Best move? | Say sorry and say he only said it because he was cross | Say it again louder | Leave the room and slam the door behind him | Say she's old-fashioned
test: little cousin asks «if u can be forgiven every time, why not do stuff?». What's the wise answer? | Because each wrong still hurts someone and you must put it right | Good point, do what you like | Because the priest will run out of forgiveness one day | Because you only get three goes
test: «said my prayers, Mum» but you didn't. Mum: «What did you pray for, love?» Best move? | Own up and say a short prayer with her | Make one up fast | Say it's private between you and God | Pretend to fall asleep
test: You drew on a hymn book last year and nobody noticed. What's still right to do? | Tell the churchwarden and help replace it | Nothing, it was last year so it's too late now | Draw on another to match | Hide the book
test: Bored again after school, the same boys call round. What's the habit-changing choice? | Go to football club or do something else | Go with them, but stay at the back of the group | Go along but don't throw anything yourself | Hide indoors and tell Mum that you're ill
test: Vicar mixes up «bless» and «mess» and the whole pew snorts. You laughed loudest. What's kind? | Say sorry to him at the door | Laugh about it all week with your mates | Copy his mix-up in the playground | Pretend it wasn't you who laughed
exam: Identify the principal teaching conveyed to a penitent who returns to confession shortly after a previous absolution. | Forgiveness does not run out, but each wrong must still be put right | A second absolution is not available | Repeated confession indicates insincerity and must therefore be refused | Absolution once given renders subsequent wrongs inconsequential
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the discharge of a firecracker at a dwelling occupied by an elderly person? | It endangers persons, animals and property and causes great distress | It constitutes a harmless jest | It is permissible during festivals, when such noise is customary | It is without consequence if no injury results
exam: It is given that a child has damaged a neighbour's porch and has been forgiven. Select the step most consistent with making amends. | Personal apology and labour towards the repair of the damage | Absence from the neighbour's vicinity until the matter is forgotten | Reliance upon the parent's payment alone | Assertion that the damage was minor
exam: State the measure recommended as most conducive to the alteration of a habit of idle mischief. | The reduction of idle time and of the company of those who encourage mischief | The avoidance of all friends whatsoever | The repetition of promises without any change in the child's circumstances | The confinement of the child indoors indefinitely
